WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.100
The European Union says soon, half of its
citizens will have been vaccinated against

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.180
COVID-19.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.080
As infection rates go down, many countries
are slowly opening up.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.350
Among them, the Czech Republic - where people
are coming back to its historic beer gardens

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.640
and evocative medieval cities.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.980
But for many families there, life is not returning
to normal.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:27.289
Their country suffered the highest death rate
in the EU – and they say that could have

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:30.050
been avoided.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.160
This is their final farewell – COVID has
taken their bookstore and Marie's husband,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:41.840
the local bookseller:
It was really sudden.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.630
I hadn't expected it would hit my husband.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:51.090
But in 9 days he was dead.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:55.460
I thought about carrying on, but ahead of
us was a series of further lockdowns, and

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.050
we couldn't have sold anything.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.570
This Shop was Jaromir Vytopil's life.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.000
He was the Czech Republic's oldest bookseller.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.360
Holding on to his profession throughout changing
political regimes, during the Prague Spring

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.740
and under Communism.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.690
When the iron curtain fell he could finally
become proud owner of his own store.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.050
Every morning before opening up he had coffee
and read the papers in this corner café.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.400
Mr. Vytopil is a legend.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.000
When you came into his shop for a book, just
a talk or a bit of advice – he always helped.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.430
It was his passion, so much was clear.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.450
But in November, with COVID raging unchecked
in the country, Jaromir fell gravely ill and

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.799
succumbed to the virus.

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:45.799
Now widow Marie and daughter Vera are picking
up the pieces.

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:52.740
Only a few boxes of books are left in the
family home:

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.189
We tried to protect ourselves from COVID,
but in a bookshop you can't stay behind the

00:01:58.189 --> 00:01:59.830
counter… when a client needs help you have
to go and find the right book for them, and

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:07.560
maybe my husband forget to put on his mask
– I think that's how it happened.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.500
But for many Czechs grief has turned into
rage.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.250
Here in Prague protesters have covered central
the Old Town Square with thousands of white

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.500
crosses, one for each victim of the pandemic.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:26.620
Activist Jacub Zevl believes government negligence
fueled the country's high death toll:

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:37.440
There were elections in the beginning of October
and obviously for PR its very bad to start

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:48.160
new restrictions for Covid, so they didn’t,
and let the epidemic grow and grow and grow.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.400
Anger against authorities is increasing, adding
to the longstanding outrage increasing over

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.459
alleged corruption, and people are taking
to the streets

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:01.629
I think the government and Prime Minister
Babis have used the pandemic to foster their

00:03:01.629 --> 00:03:04.430
own interests.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.150
I think the Prime Minister has put his own
election campaign before he health of his

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:15.129
citizens, that makes me really angry
Back in Pehlrimov Marie has only family photos

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:24.069
to remind her of a long marriage and partnership
– all that Covid has taken from her.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.860
The clients grieved with us, and that was
something really special.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.790
They cried and brought us flowers.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:36.650
It was such a sad goodbye.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:42.500
For so many Czech families goodbyes came to
soon, amid the symbols of grief for a country

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.900
that lost part of its soul.

